{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>A torn animal I did not, etc.</b> It is difficult: how can one justify speaking of a thing that was torn with the language of something brought? Further, why did he repeat, saying <b>אָנֹכִי אֲחַטֶּנָּה מִיָּדִי תְּבַקְשֶׁנָּה</b> — if he would pay for it, what place is there to demand it from his hand?" "<b>Indeed,</b> Jacob's intention is to say: both a matter for which he is liable according to law and a matter for which he is exempt, Laban took from him. And he was precise in saying ״טְרֵפָה״ with reference to a wolf, and he was precise in saying ״לֹא הֵבֵאתִי״ with reference to an animal crushed by a lion. And so you will find in the language of the Sages in tractate Metzia (Bava Metzia 93a), and this is their language: The Tanna'im taught: A watchman who left his flock and came to the city, and a lion came and crushed [a beast], a wolf came and tore [a beast] — end of quote. They used the language of טְרֵפָה for a wolf, because it tears and flees out of the fright it causes, unlike the lion, which crushes [its prey] in the place where it finds it and eats it there, leaving behind the skin and the dung. And so too Jacob alluded to two matters in saying ״טְרֵפָה״ with reference to a wolf, ״לֹא הֵבֵאתִי״ with reference to a lion that leaves skin and dung, which it leaves behind in its crushing. And he said <b>אָנֹכִי אֲחַטֶּנָּה</b> with reference to the category for which he is liable — that is, the tearing of a wolf, such as a single wolf, which is not an unavoidable accident, and a paid watchman is liable (Bava Metzia 72b). And he said <b>מִיָּדִי תְּבַקְשֶׁנָּה</b> with reference to the second category, which is the crushing of a lion, for which there is no liability upon Jacob to pay, since it is an unavoidable accident and he is exempt — except that the wicked man would denounce him and demand even this from Jacob. And according to this, a spirit of holy inspiration was cast in the words of Rashi, of blessed memory, who wrote, and this is his language: ״טְרֵפָה״ etc., by means of a lion and a wolf — end of quote; and it accords with our words and is correct." "And his saying <b>גְּנֻבְתִי יוֹם</b> etc. — he further intended regarding the matter of damages caused by men, that he would pay both for what a paid watchman is liable and for what he is not liable. And that is his saying <b>גְּנֻבְתִי יוֹם וּגְנֻבְתִי לָיְלָה</b> — meaning, גְּנֻבְתִי — theft reached me even on the side of exemption."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {1 "The citation of Bava Metzia 93a appears to refer to the discussion there rather than to the exact wording cited, which the Or HaChaim paraphrases in his own voice."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:31.836022494Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}